WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:28.380
Thank you very much, Chairman. As you said,
this paper sets out an update on the five

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.850
commitments that are in our business plan
for this year, so I will just highlight a

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:38.610
few of the key things that have been delivered
over the last few months, and certainly since

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:44.180
our last meeting.
One of the things I wanted to really point

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:49.830
out about commitment one is that the work
we have done on the care and treatment reviews

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:55.620
has had, I think, a significant impact in
raising awareness, and also in really focusing

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:02.500
on what is happening for people who are inpatients.
To date, we have done just over 1,800 of the

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:08.280
reviews, and importantly we have supported
26 regional and national events that have

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.890
actually meant that we have been able to access
over 350 stakeholders. We have been able to

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.260
do that with experts by experience, people
with learning disabilities’ families, talking

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:26.049
through how those should be done and how they
can best be carried out. We have also done

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:29.780
a review of the involvement of our experts
by experience, and I was talking to some of

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:36.260
the people with a learning disability who
have been part of those reviews, and their

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.440
response has been very positive. They felt
that they have been able to contribute something

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.840
very meaningful and worthwhile to the reviews,
they have been able to engage with people

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.940
who are inpatients, and they have been able
to identify some of the key things that are

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:55.650
either stopping people from being discharged
or some of the behaviours that may have affected

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:01.320
that. So that has been incredibly positive,
and we will continue to roll those out; particularly

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:08.069
targeting children under the age of 18, which
is one of our more recent priorities.

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:11.909
Annual health checks and extending the offer
of personal budgets are in the paper; also

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:16.360
very important factors, and I can talk about
that a bit later if you want me to. Around

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:24.150
commitment two, we have specifically worked
with the Association of Directors of Children’s

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.439
Services and the Department of Education,
particularly to ensure that the care and treatment

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:33.510
reviews we do for children under the age of
18 are very closely linked with the Education,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:38.549
Health and Care Plans which are part of the
Care Act. I think those two pieces of work,

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:45.749
plus looking very specifically at those children
who are in 52-week residential schools, is

00:02:45.749 --> 00:02:53.029
really important. We know that there is a
link between discharge from a residential

00:02:53.029 --> 00:02:59.200
school and subsequent admission into an inpatient
setting, and what we are looking at as part

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.739
of the review with those is to make sure that
any discharge or any leaving a 52-week residential

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:11.239
school actually means that young people can
be supported to live in a community setting.

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:19.639
So that has been a very important and very
helpful piece of work that we have done.

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:25.169
The key thing, though, that I wanted to talk
about is our commitment to close inappropriate

00:03:25.169 --> 00:03:30.329
facilities and commission more appropriate
community-based services, and I have talked

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:38.480
about that on many occasions at the Board.
I think it is probably worth just identifying:

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.599
we looked at the number of NHS inpatient beds
that we have had. Since the beginning of 2010/2011

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:53.730
there has been around a 40% reduction in NHS
inpatient beds, and in quarter four 2013/2014

00:03:53.730 --> 00:04:02.430
to quarter four 2014/2015 a further 12% reduction,
and we are seeing a gradual change in commissioning

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:07.859
intentions and a reduction. However, I think
most people will say that is not fast enough,

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:12.239
and we know that that is a commitment that
we have made and others have made. The work

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:19.470
we have done over the last few months is to
create a new service model which will be launched

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:24.000
by Monday.
We have also identified the five fast-track

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:31.730
areas, and those fast-track areas are a similar
approach to the vanguards. They started slightly

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:39.760
later and they are expected to give us some
significant detail much sooner. We have identified

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.180
some support for those; they have financial
support, they have people support. They are

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:52.750
really working at pace to look at how we can
take the new service model, how we can look

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:59.960
at how we can commission and provide additional
services in a community setting, and what

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.410
that will then mean for the number of beds
that we think we need.

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:11.230
You will have heard me talk here before about
the variation in inpatient provision. If you

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:14.660
look at the number of beds per thousand of
the population in the South or London, it

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:22.160
is a lot less than it is if you live in the
Midlands or the North, so one of the things

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.570
we want to do is to make sure that we reduce
that variation and that there is a similar

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:32.200
approach to commissioning and provision across
the country. We expect by October therefore

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.930
to have a more detailed national plan that
will build on the work of the fast-tracks

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.060
which are deliberately based in the areas
which have the highest number of inpatient

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:49.750
beds. We will use the new service model, which
focuses a lot on proactive, preventative care,

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:54.390
empowerment of people with a learning disability,
using personal budgets, supporting families

00:05:54.390 --> 00:06:00.460
to be cared for at home or at a place that
they choose to call home; enabling them to

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:06.530
have access to specialist multi-disciplinary
support in a community setting. Then when

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:11.350
we do look at hospital inpatient stay, which
will be needed for some people for some time,

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:17.280
we will look that that is very high quality,
as short as possible, and anybody who is an

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:24.800
inpatient will have a very clear plan for
discharge.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:30.470
The fourth area: we said we wanted to establish
a national learning disability mortality review,

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:38.000
and very recently we have just published that.
We have, through the Healthcare Quality Improvement

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.170
Partnership and the University of Bristol,
announced the first national programme to

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:46.950
review premature mortality in learning disability.
It is the first time it has been done in the

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:51.400
world. It is a three-year programme, and we
would expect that to provide us with some

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:57.420
very useful information and actually a real
focus on how we can improve premature mortality

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.830
and make sure that the access to physical
health and physical health support is certainly

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:08.590
as strong and as good as it absolutely needs
to be.

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:14.280
Commitment five was particularly around data.
When I took over this programme last year,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.810
one of the things that was very clear was
that we did not have good enough information

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:24.520
to be able to give us the facts we needed
to be able to monitor progress, and we had

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:32.200
put in a lot of work and quite a lot of manual
workarounds in order to be able to give us

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:37.639
as much data and as much information as we
can. We have now transferred the Assuring

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:43.320
Transformation data through to the HSCIC.
It has taken a little while to bed in, but

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:48.090
what we are finding now is that we are getting
monthly data and the quality of that data

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:58.590
is much better, and we will expect that to
continue as we carry on. That means that we

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.770
have a much stronger grip on the number of
admissions, the number of discharges, the

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:08.169
number of people who are in inpatient beds,
so we can monitor that on a much tighter basis,

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:13.450
which I think has been really helpful.
The last thing I just wanted to talk about

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:19.230
was the stakeholder involvement. You will
have heard me talk at the last meeting around

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:24.520
the different groups that we have set up.
We have a significant stakeholder reference

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:31.500
group which is jointly chaired by Professor
Tony Holland and Scott Watkins, a leading

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:38.240
academic in the field and a person with a
learning disability. They have been very,

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:43.529
very strongly involved with multiple stakeholders
in the development of the service model. That

00:08:43.529 --> 00:08:50.389
has been incredibly positive in the input
that they have had. We also held a learning

00:08:50.389 --> 00:08:57.360
disability awareness week recently, where
we had an event in both London and Leeds.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:03.069
Those of you who were able to attend that
I think will say it was incredibly powerful.

00:09:03.069 --> 00:09:11.660
We had some actors acting out what it meant
to be a carer looking after somebody who did

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:17.249
not have the appropriate physical health checks.
We had people with a learning disability describing

00:09:17.249 --> 00:09:22.110
what their experiences were, what the impact
for them was on being listened to and being

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:29.370
responded to and being given opportunities
to live a normal life. That was really good,

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.689
and we have great commitment from both people
with learning disabilities and their families

00:09:32.689 --> 00:09:38.459
to continue to work with us through their
stakeholder engagement forum.

00:09:38.459 --> 00:09:45.079
Again, I am really pleased to say that having
talked about it for a while, we now have in

00:09:45.079 --> 00:09:50.689
post three people working for NHS England
with a learning disability; one working in

00:09:50.689 --> 00:09:56.279
our Improving Lives team and two working in
our Public Engagement team. They are working

00:09:56.279 --> 00:10:01.740
alongside co workers to support them, and
we are about to recruit a fourth person into

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.670
our Improving Lives team. It is one of our
commitments to really support people with

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:11.910
a learning disability to get access to jobs,
because that really does have a massive impact

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:19.689
on their health and wellbeing and their ability
to live as normal a life as possible.

00:10:19.689 --> 00:10:25.959
I wanted you to consider the update, to see
what progress we are making. The next big

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:32.689
step will be the publication of the service
model, and that should be by Monday. I will

00:10:32.689 --> 00:10:37.050
end there, and I am very, very happy to take
any questions.

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:41.730
Thank you very much, Jane. You know the Board
are very keen to track progress on this, and

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:47.990
to bring it back regularly to our meeting
so that we can see how we are proceeding.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:57.410
I think this is a real step forward. Well
done. I really do think it shows a clear intention

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:04.660
to reshape the vision for services for people
with learning disabilities, all 1.5 million

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:09.889
of them, and I think you should be congratulated.
I think there is a theme for the things that

00:11:09.889 --> 00:11:15.160
I say in this meeting, which is: I am really
interested in the impact of this work on the

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:20.600
culture of NHS England and the things that
we do, and what the challenges are in actually

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:24.869
making some of this real. So if we are going
to employ people with learning disabilities

00:11:24.869 --> 00:11:30.990
in NHS England to do this, to help with this,
and if we are engaging with people with learning

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:37.670
disabilities in the monitoring of the service
model roll-out, and if we are engaging with

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:47.410
the relatives and carers of such people, then
it should be uncomfortable for us, so I am

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:50.259
interested in how we know that and we manage
it.

00:11:50.259 --> 00:11:57.490
I guess the other bit that I am interested
in is: those people with learning disabilities

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:05.589
who actually are a long way from employment
because of the nature of their learning disability,

00:12:05.589 --> 00:12:12.689
the people who are non-communicative. So I
would be quite interested in what we are doing

00:12:12.689 --> 00:12:19.040
around that. You did not specifically refer
to it, but particularly those communities

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:24.509
that tend to be outside the framework of consultation
when we think about people with learning disabilities;

00:12:24.509 --> 00:12:28.699
so ethnic minority communities, for instance.
I know there has been some work around this.

00:12:28.699 --> 00:12:34.329
I think I was present at one of the events
that took place with people with learning

00:12:34.329 --> 00:12:37.699
disabilities, where we talked about health
checks, and I think the point that was being

00:12:37.699 --> 00:12:43.879
made during that meeting was: actually, it
should not be an option for primary care providers

00:12:43.879 --> 00:12:47.839
or any other provider not to provide health
checks, and it seemed like it was. And there

00:12:47.839 --> 00:12:54.170
was a view that, well, why are we commissioning
services that do not do health checks? Why

00:12:54.170 --> 00:13:01.259
is it an option? It seemed unreasonable for
us to pay for something that is not happening

00:13:01.259 --> 00:13:08.309
but that should. I just wonder whether you
might comment on that, but I do think this

00:13:08.309 --> 00:13:13.670
is a step change, potentially.
You are right: annual health checks should

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:19.490
be happening, there is no doubt about that.
Again, the issue we found is that there is

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:23.999
quite a lot of variation, so we are looking
at using the data that we are now able to

00:13:23.999 --> 00:13:31.649
get and actually identifying that, and also
working with primary care colleagues to help

00:13:31.649 --> 00:13:36.809
understand the best way of doing them. Some
of the reasons they do not happen is because

00:13:36.809 --> 00:13:41.209
the reasonable adjustments that are needed
to support people to have an annual health

00:13:41.209 --> 00:13:47.279
check are quite difficult to do, and that
was what was so graphically demonstrated in

00:13:47.279 --> 00:13:55.949
the performance that we saw by the company
called Operating Theatre. It is partly supporting

00:13:55.949 --> 00:14:01.360
primary care colleagues to be able to do that
with the appropriate reasonable adjustments,

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:06.959
as well as actually almost holding a mirror
up in terms of the variation and the difference

00:14:06.959 --> 00:14:11.350
in take-up, which I think also helps.
Your point about whether it feels uncomfortable

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:18.619
is, I think, really well made. Yes, I have
been involved in this programme for about

00:14:18.619 --> 00:14:33.129
15 or 16 months, and it has been at times
very uncomfortable, and rightly so. This is

00:14:33.129 --> 00:14:38.949
not just NHS England; I have spent a long
time in the NHS, and this really is one of

00:14:38.949 --> 00:14:44.949
the first times when I have seen a really
joined-up piece of work where we are alongside

00:14:44.949 --> 00:14:50.779
ADAS, we are alongside LGA, we have CQC involved,
we have Health Education England involved,

00:14:50.779 --> 00:14:58.420
we have patients, carers, families, providers
and commissioners involved. It is difficult,

00:14:58.420 --> 00:15:03.160
because there are lots of different ways of
working, different cultures, but it really

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:08.999
does feel like the vision and the commitment
is there by everybody who we speak to, and

00:15:08.999 --> 00:15:13.360
everybody is committed to breaking down those
things that are difficult. They are difficult

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:18.709
at times, and it is not an easy programme.
If it was, it would have been done a lot longer

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:24.920
ago. So although sometimes I do feel uncomfortable
and some of my colleagues do, we know it is

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:28.720
the right thing and it is the right thing
to feel uncomfortable, because people are

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:34.259
actually pushing us to say, ‘So why have
you not done this?’ or, ‘Why can you not

00:15:34.259 --> 00:15:41.809
do that?’ Just having that type of push
and people working with us to do the right

00:15:41.809 --> 00:15:49.029
thing does help, but what was very good – and
this happened a week or so when we were in

00:15:49.029 --> 00:15:55.059
a big programme board with DH colleagues – was
when somebody who runs one of the value and

00:15:55.059 --> 00:16:00.239
family forums said, ‘I have been involved
in this and I have seen the engagement, and

00:16:00.239 --> 00:16:03.980
it has been really good, and I have been really
happy with the way this is being run by NHS

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:09.929
England for this particular part of the programme.’
Still very keen that we go further faster.

00:16:09.929 --> 00:16:15.600
That positive feedback is also very motivating
for the staff that are working hard on this.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:22.129
And the severe learning disability?
The bit about employment, again, is very well

00:16:22.129 --> 00:16:28.509
made. What we are looking at, it is about
choice, so people making a choice if they

00:16:28.509 --> 00:16:33.019
want to work and trying to find a way of enabling
them to do that. However, for many people

00:16:33.019 --> 00:16:37.949
it will be having access to a personal budget
that allows them to do other things that are

00:16:37.949 --> 00:16:45.279
meaningful for them, and I think that is what
is important. It is not saying, ‘This individual

00:16:45.279 --> 00:16:49.970
is in a wheelchair, cannot communicate, therefore
cannot be given an option to do something,’

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:54.129
and we have lots of individual examples where
by giving somebody a personal health budget

00:16:54.129 --> 00:16:59.720
or a personal budget, they can choose to do
different things and to do it well, and to

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:05.040
be able to access different communication
aids that will help them be able to communicate.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.790
So it is back down to the point that I have
made quite a few times: there is no one blanket

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:14.410
approach. This has to be very personal and
very personalised to individual needs and

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:21.420
individual desires.
Can I just add one thing, just to build on

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:29.100
that point: on the one hand, this is obviously
about massively improving service for people.

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:35.940
However, I just want to reflect on a broader
point which is that we have been working very

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:42.160
closely with the team that has been working
with Jane on much broader ways of empowering

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:46.980
people with learning disabilities on a whole
range of other fronts, and I just wanted to

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:50.940
bring to the Board’s attention I think another
big milestone here – an important breakthrough,

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:55.010
again, working with colleagues with learning
disabilities and other disabilities – which

00:17:55.010 --> 00:18:00.320
has been the publication of the Accessible
Information Standard. This sets a mandatory

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:06.240
requirement on NHS organisations to take much
more seriously the vitally empowering opportunity

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:10.780
of publishing information in ways which everybody
can understand, and not publishing documents

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:14.990
which are literally unintelligible to many
people who are engaging with the service.

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:19.070
I just wanted to pay tribute to Jane’s leadership.
It is not just about service improvement;

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:24.560
it is actually a much broader agenda around
actually supporting people who are people,

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:28.010
to maximise the opportunities they have, and
the Accessible Information Standard after

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:32.810
years of negotiation was finally published
because we are working very differently with

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:36.820
patient groups, advocates, particularly with
those learning disabilities. I just wanted

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:41.190
to reflect on that.
Thank you, that is a very telling point. I

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:47.550
think what is so interesting about your paper
today, Jane, is the very broad view that it

00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:53.540
takes of the need to support people with learning
disabilities across the whole sphere of what

00:18:53.540 --> 00:19:03.360
occurs within the NHS, and I think particularly
your commitment towards October for the whole

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:10.400
transformation plan, the bid closures, etc.,
is something that we want to follow with great

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:10.820
interest.

